nftables: meta mark set, sets of keys and anonymous sets

What:
- "meta mark set" sets a packet's mark from a register, as
  net/netfilter/nft_meta.c:nft_meta_set_eval() does, instead of failing with
  EOPNOTSUPP.
- A set without data (no NFT_SET_MAP), whose elements are single keys, is
  created, on the same hash backend as maps, as Linux's nft_set_hash; sets of
  intervals, with timeouts or updated by rules are still refused with
  EOPNOTSUPP.
- A set named with one %d, as nft names its anonymous sets ("__set%d"), gets
  the lowest number no set of its table has, as
  net/netfilter/nf_tables_api.c:nf_tables_set_alloc_name() does; a name with
  another conversion, or too long for NFT_SET_MAX_ANONLEN, is EINVAL. Before,
  the name was kept as it is, so a second anonymous set in a table failed as
  existing.
- A lookup and a set element list find a set created in the same batch by
  its transaction ID (NFTA_LOOKUP_SET_ID, NFTA_SET_ELEM_LIST_SET_ID), as
  nft_set_lookup_global() does, instead of taking the ID for the set's handle.
- An anonymous set that no rule is bound to at the end of a batch, once the
  rule bound to it is deleted, is removed, as Linux removes it with its rule
  (nf_tables_deactivate_set()).

Why: netavark 1.14 and 1.17, whose nftables driver Debian 13's and Fedora
43's Podman use, write "meta mark set meta mark | 0x2000" to mark connections
for masquerading and "meta l4proto { udp, tcp }" for DNS. The first failed,
and an anonymous set failed to be created: "Could not process rule:
Operation not supported" ("only map sets are supported yet"); once it could
be, the second container's batch failed, because the anonymous set of the
first container's rule outlived the rule and kept the name.

Not done: Linux refuses a batch that leaves a new anonymous set unbound
(EINVAL); gVisor removes the set at the end of the batch instead.

Upstream: none found (master still has only map sets, b/505409691); drop
this patch when upstream has these.

Verified:
- The new unit tests TestEvaluateMetaSetMark, TestNewSetOfKeys and
  TestAnonymousSets (names, transaction IDs, the end of a batch, a deleted
  rule, invalid names) pass with the rest of
  //pkg/tcpip/nftables:nftables_test in gVisor's builder container.
  socket_netlink_netfilter_test passes on runsc with --TESTONLY-nftables
  --net-raw (268 tests), as on ALE's 0.1.2.
- netavark 1.14.0 (Podman 5.4.2, Debian 13) and 1.17.2 (Podman 5.8.9,
  Fedora 43) in sandboxes under runsc-nested set up and tore down their rules
  for container after container, and published ports.

